            "text": "Solves A x = b by least squares: finds the x that makes A x come as close to b as possible, when no x can make them exactly equal.\n\nThat is the situation whenever you fit a model to measurements. Five noisy points cannot all lie on one line, but some line passes closest to all of them - the one whose squared misses add up to the least. Write the model as a matrix (one row per measurement), and lstsq gives the line, plane, scale-and-offset or calibration that fits best.\n\nThe matrix holds what you know about each measurement, and x is what you want to find. To fit y = slope * t + offset to points (t, y), each row of A is t 1, b holds the y values, and x comes out as slope offset. Add a t*t column and it fits a curve instead.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.lstsq\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nA matrix:\nThe 2-D matrix, one row per equation (measurement). Every input solves and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nThe right-hand side: one value per row of A (or one row of values per row of A, to solve several fits at once). Held: send it first.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nx:\nThe best-fitting solution: one value per column of A.\n\nresiduals:\nThe sum of the squared misses: 0 for a perfect fit, larger the worse the fit. It comes back empty when there are no more equations than unknowns, or when the columns of A are not independent.\n\nrank:\nHow many independent columns A has. Fewer than its number of columns means some unknowns cannot be told apart (two columns that are the same), and x is only one of many equally good answers. Sent first, then residuals, then x.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFive points measured at t = 0 1 2 3 4: y = 1.1 2.9 5.2 6.8 9.1 - roughly a straight line. Each row of A is t 1. The fit gives a slope of about 2 and an offset of about 1: y = 2t + 1, with a small residual for the noise. Change a y value and click the b message to see the line move. The round nodes only tidy the numbers for reading.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.solve solves a square system exactly. np.linalg.pinv is the matrix that lstsq effectively uses: pinv of A times b is the same x."
        },
